**Clarify.** What does `score` look like, for example "25-20" (points for team1 and team2)? Do teams accumulate points, or does a win add a fixed number? Ties for first place: lexicographic, or first to reach? Can a team's total ever decrease (corrections)? I assume points accumulate from the score string, totals never decrease, and ties go to the alphabetically smaller name.

**Key insight.** If totals only increase, only the two teams in the current match can overtake the leader. So keep a `HashMap<String, Integer> totals` and a running `leader`. After each `addMatch`, update the two totals and compare each against the leader. That makes `addMatch` O(1) and `findFirst` **O(1)**, which satisfies "less than O(n)".

```java
class GameTracker {
    private final Map<String,Integer> total = new HashMap<>();
    private String leader = null;

    void addMatch(String t1, String t2, String score) {
        String[] p = score.split("-");
        int s1 = Integer.parseInt(p[0].trim()), s2 = Integer.parseInt(p[1].trim());
        bump(t1, s1); bump(t2, s2);
    }
    private void bump(String t, int pts) {
        int v = total.merge(t, pts, Integer::sum);
        if (leader == null) { leader = t; return; }
        int lv = total.get(leader);
        if (v > lv || (v == lv && t.compareTo(leader) < 0)) leader = t;
    }
    String findFirst() { return leader; }
}
```

**If totals can decrease or be corrected,** the running leader is not enough. Use a `TreeSet` ordered by (total desc, name) and remove and reinsert on each update: `addMatch` O(log n), `findFirst` O(1) or O(log n). Or a max-heap with **lazy deletion**: push `(total, team)` on every update and, in `findFirst`, pop stale entries whose total differs from the map. That is O(log n) amortised.

**Edge cases:** no matches yet (return null or throw), malformed score, same team on both sides, negative points, and thread safety if matches arrive concurrently (synchronise or use `ConcurrentHashMap` with care, since the leader update must be atomic).
